1
00:00:03,503 --> 00:00:05,464
Pre-war standards
never would have seen me

2
00:00:05,547 --> 00:00:07,257
promoted to general like this.

3
00:00:07,341 --> 00:00:09,218
I guess those stars aren't worth
what they used to be.

4
00:00:11,887 --> 00:00:13,513
We can keep
winning these victories

5
00:00:13,597 --> 00:00:14,932
at the tactical level,

6
00:00:15,015 --> 00:00:17,643
but it doesn't move the needle
strategically.

7
00:00:18,352 --> 00:00:20,520
Why is he being so aggressive?

8
00:00:20,604 --> 00:00:23,440
What could possibly be
going through Rudersdorf's head?

9
00:00:26,860 --> 00:00:28,028
I have a letter.

10
00:00:28,111 --> 00:00:29,988
Prepare a messenger for me
at once.

11
00:00:34,910 --> 00:00:35,953
[sighs]

12
00:00:36,036 --> 00:00:38,997
I say, but now that I'm here,

13
00:00:39,081 --> 00:00:40,165
what do I write?

14
00:00:40,791 --> 00:00:42,709
What is there to say?

15
00:00:44,753 --> 00:00:45,879
My best friend.

16
00:00:47,631 --> 00:00:49,216
[♪ opening theme song playing]

17
00:02:17,554 --> 00:02:18,930
♪

18
00:02:30,692 --> 00:02:31,777
[KURT] This is it.

19
00:02:32,903 --> 00:02:34,446
We're running out of time.

20
00:02:47,959 --> 00:02:49,252
What brings you home?

21
00:02:49,336 --> 00:02:50,754
Did something happen there?

22
00:02:51,380 --> 00:02:54,049
We were forced to evacuate
the entire border, dear.

23
00:02:55,175 --> 00:02:57,761
You didn't see it in
the military's official paper?

24
00:02:59,137 --> 00:03:01,723
No, I must have missed that.

25
00:03:02,307 --> 00:03:05,185
[leaves rustling]

26
00:03:06,186 --> 00:03:08,021
So, it's been one year.

27
00:03:09,940 --> 00:03:11,233
I'm sorry, dear.

28
00:03:11,983 --> 00:03:13,485
No need to be sorry, Dad.

29
00:03:14,152 --> 00:03:16,279
We may have only been married
five days,

30
00:03:16,905 --> 00:03:19,116
[chuckles] but I was happy...

31
00:03:20,075 --> 00:03:21,952
...and I was blessed
with a child.

32
00:03:23,078 --> 00:03:24,037
You know...

33
00:03:24,830 --> 00:03:27,541
every day I replay his words.

34
00:03:27,624 --> 00:03:29,960
If something happened... I mean,

35
00:03:30,710 --> 00:03:32,212
if he really died for us...

36
00:03:34,923 --> 00:03:37,300
...then at least
his death meant something, yeah?

37
00:03:45,267 --> 00:03:47,894
To you who volunteered,
we celebrate!

38
00:03:47,978 --> 00:03:51,314
You are the chosen ones!
The future of the Empire!

39
00:03:51,398 --> 00:03:52,774
You weren't conscripted...

40
00:03:52,858 --> 00:03:55,026
[MAN 1]
Such wonderful patriotism.

41
00:03:55,694 --> 00:03:56,862
[MAN 2] Can you believe it?

42
00:03:56,945 --> 00:03:59,156
There are young people
who don't volunteer.

43
00:03:59,239 --> 00:04:00,866
[MAN 3]
Just wait till after the war.

44
00:04:00,949 --> 00:04:03,702
Good luck getting a job
if you shirked your duty now.

45
00:04:03,785 --> 00:04:04,870
[MAN 4] That's right.

46
00:04:04,953 --> 00:04:06,746
Why would we want them
in any power

47
00:04:06,830 --> 00:04:08,039
when they abandoned
their brothers today?

48
00:04:08,123 --> 00:04:08,999
[crowd clapping]

49
00:04:10,208 --> 00:04:12,335
[KURT] This here is the voice
of the people.

50
00:04:13,587 --> 00:04:16,173
How can we tell them
it was all for nothing?

51
00:04:17,466 --> 00:04:19,885
[engine sputtering]

52
00:04:24,931 --> 00:04:26,391
[explosion]

53
00:04:27,642 --> 00:04:29,853
We got bombed in broad daylight?

54
00:04:29,936 --> 00:04:31,062
-Impossible!
-[planes roaring]

55
00:04:36,026 --> 00:04:37,360
Status report.

56
00:04:37,444 --> 00:04:39,488
General, things don't look good.

57
00:04:40,155 --> 00:04:42,741
They dropped most of the bombs
on our ceremonial grounds.

58
00:04:42,824 --> 00:04:44,618
The damage done was minimal,
but--

59
00:04:44,701 --> 00:04:47,662
The real attack was the hit
to the army's morale.

60
00:04:47,746 --> 00:04:49,831
They used
their new high-speed bombers

61
00:04:49,915 --> 00:04:51,791
so our planes
couldn't catch up to them.

62
00:04:51,875 --> 00:04:53,960
There was no opportunity
to intercept.

63
00:04:54,044 --> 00:04:56,296
It's not as if a bomb
weighs nothing at all.

64
00:04:56,379 --> 00:04:58,715
How could our lightweight
fighters not catch up to them?

65
00:04:58,798 --> 00:05:01,009
[ERICH] Their cruising speed
is exceptional.

66
00:05:01,092 --> 00:05:02,052
By the time they hit it,

67
00:05:02,344 --> 00:05:03,970
there was almost no chance
for us to react.

68
00:05:04,554 --> 00:05:05,972
And with so few planes,

69
00:05:06,056 --> 00:05:08,433
it's all the more difficult
to track, I'm told.

70
00:05:08,517 --> 00:05:09,601
[sighs]

71
00:05:10,519 --> 00:05:12,062
How come
we couldn't get an alert out

72
00:05:12,145 --> 00:05:14,523
before they dropped
their payload on our heads?

73
00:05:14,606 --> 00:05:17,275
They've been sending out
decoy bombers for a while now.

74
00:05:17,359 --> 00:05:18,527
Small fleets whose only purpose

75
00:05:18,610 --> 00:05:20,028
was to distract
our interceptors.

76
00:05:20,695 --> 00:05:22,697
This was likely deemed
another such case.

77
00:05:23,406 --> 00:05:25,659
We were so scared
of being the boy who cried wolf,

78
00:05:25,742 --> 00:05:28,036
we ignored the wolf
that was really there.

79
00:05:28,119 --> 00:05:30,664
We need a way to ensure
this doesn't happen again.

80
00:05:31,331 --> 00:05:33,416
They're innovating faster
than us.

81
00:05:33,500 --> 00:05:36,002
We can't allow this disgrace
to continue.

82
00:05:36,086 --> 00:05:37,003
[ERICH] Sir!

83
00:05:40,632 --> 00:05:43,635
[KURT] That's right.
We can't fall behind.

84
00:05:44,302 --> 00:05:47,097
Our army, the Empire,

85
00:05:47,889 --> 00:05:50,350
we cannot be left in the dust.

86
00:05:51,560 --> 00:05:53,562
[scribbling]

87
00:05:56,064 --> 00:05:57,732
♪

88
00:06:06,032 --> 00:06:07,450
It's not over yet.

89
00:06:12,455 --> 00:06:15,625
That idiot Zettour
won't be happy with me for this.

90
00:06:16,918 --> 00:06:18,878
It may be rash,

91
00:06:18,962 --> 00:06:20,839
but it's better than giving up.

92
00:06:21,548 --> 00:06:22,716
We'll fight

93
00:06:23,341 --> 00:06:25,468
for the glory of the Empire.

94
00:06:26,803 --> 00:06:28,513
♪

95
00:06:42,444 --> 00:06:43,737
[Hans exhales]

96
00:06:43,820 --> 00:06:45,697
Don't tell me
you came all the way out here

97
00:06:45,780 --> 00:06:46,823
just to see me.

98
00:06:47,574 --> 00:06:49,451
Did you miss your old pal
that badly?

99
00:06:50,619 --> 00:06:52,120
So what if I did?

100
00:06:52,746 --> 00:06:55,123
Try to relax. I was only joking.

101
00:06:55,749 --> 00:06:57,918
-Have you read the plan?
-Yes.

102
00:06:58,793 --> 00:07:00,629
Frankly,
it raised some questions.

103
00:07:01,296 --> 00:07:02,505
To start...

104
00:07:02,589 --> 00:07:03,632
are you mad?

105
00:07:04,257 --> 00:07:05,592
I'm perfectly sane.

106
00:07:05,675 --> 00:07:08,595
I'm trying to win the victory
our Empire needs.

107
00:07:08,678 --> 00:07:10,055
-You see the slop...
-[KURT] We need to secure

108
00:07:10,347 --> 00:07:12,182
-air superiority in the west.
-...they're serving us today?

109
00:07:12,265 --> 00:07:14,684
If we can do that
and take down Ildoa...

110
00:07:14,768 --> 00:07:16,269
-Maybe we'd get better...
-...we can still win.

111
00:07:16,353 --> 00:07:17,312
...if we were dogs!

112
00:07:17,395 --> 00:07:19,439
[KURT]
It's the only chance left.

113
00:07:21,107 --> 00:07:22,442
A brilliant plan.

114
00:07:22,525 --> 00:07:24,235
We don't even need aircraft

115
00:07:24,319 --> 00:07:26,947
since we'll ride all the flying
pigs you're imagining.

116
00:07:28,573 --> 00:07:32,160
The military needs to take
complete control from here.

117
00:07:32,243 --> 00:07:33,745
That's how we'll make it happen.

118
00:07:34,537 --> 00:07:35,872
This is possible.

119
00:07:35,955 --> 00:07:37,540
-Hmm.
-Just think,

120
00:07:37,624 --> 00:07:40,168
we have the talent in our ranks,
the resolve.

121
00:07:40,794 --> 00:07:42,629
We can't hesitate now.

122
00:07:42,712 --> 00:07:45,215
We were hoping to resolve this
through diplomacy.

123
00:07:45,298 --> 00:07:46,716
You agreed to that,

124
00:07:46,800 --> 00:07:48,343
or do I need to
remind you again?

125
00:07:48,426 --> 00:07:50,887
We tried and failed.
Look where it got us.

126
00:07:50,971 --> 00:07:53,348
Don't be dense. It's just begun.

127
00:07:53,431 --> 00:07:54,766
You think you can negotiate

128
00:07:54,849 --> 00:07:56,643
through those ridiculous
conditions?

129
00:07:57,185 --> 00:07:59,020
The first step
was getting them to the table

130
00:07:59,104 --> 00:08:01,356
to give us those conditions
in the first place.

131
00:08:02,065 --> 00:08:03,274
And now...

132
00:08:03,358 --> 00:08:04,859
we lose a million.

133
00:08:04,943 --> 00:08:06,194
No, ten million.

134
00:08:06,277 --> 00:08:08,154
We let more of our men die.

135
00:08:08,238 --> 00:08:09,739
And when hardly anyone's left,

136
00:08:09,823 --> 00:08:11,658
even the dumbest
among them will talk.

137
00:08:12,283 --> 00:08:13,243
Don't be ridiculous.

138
00:08:13,910 --> 00:08:15,620
We've already lost far too many.

139
00:08:16,287 --> 00:08:19,082
What's the point of sending
more of our soldiers out to die?

140
00:08:19,165 --> 00:08:20,375
Haven't we shed enough blood?

141
00:08:20,458 --> 00:08:23,128
Good. You still have
some sanity left in you.

142
00:08:23,211 --> 00:08:26,256
So then, why do you want to
give up on diplomacy?!

143
00:08:26,339 --> 00:08:28,508
[KURT] Diplomacy gets harder
as we become weaker.

144
00:08:28,591 --> 00:08:29,467
There's no time!

145
00:08:30,093 --> 00:08:32,762
We can fight. We can bring
our tactical superiority

146
00:08:32,846 --> 00:08:35,473
to the discussions!
But the more time we waste,

147
00:08:35,557 --> 00:08:37,934
the closer we get
to losing even that strength!

148
00:08:38,018 --> 00:08:40,311
So what? That's your whole plan?

149
00:08:40,395 --> 00:08:42,480
Hit them before we get
too weak to move.

150
00:08:42,564 --> 00:08:44,190
-Exactly!
-Absurd.

151
00:08:44,274 --> 00:08:47,026
Sacrificing the strategic
for the tactical.

152
00:08:47,110 --> 00:08:49,446
[KURT] We can't let ourselves
fall further behind.

153
00:08:50,530 --> 00:08:52,532
These drastic measures
are all we have left.

154
00:08:52,615 --> 00:08:53,616
[grunts]

155
00:08:55,076 --> 00:08:56,035
Hmm.

156
00:08:57,162 --> 00:08:58,121
You think so?

157
00:08:58,788 --> 00:09:01,166
And you call yourself
a strategy officer?

158
00:09:01,249 --> 00:09:02,167
[KURT] What?

159
00:09:02,250 --> 00:09:04,627
Your masters rang a bell,

160
00:09:04,711 --> 00:09:06,629
so you can't help but drool.

161
00:09:06,713 --> 00:09:08,840
I thought you were more
than just a dog.

162
00:09:08,923 --> 00:09:10,258
How dare you!

163
00:09:10,341 --> 00:09:12,385
If my words aren't enough
to make you understand

164
00:09:12,469 --> 00:09:13,511
then maybe-- [grunts]

165
00:09:13,595 --> 00:09:15,180
Look at reality, man!

166
00:09:15,263 --> 00:09:16,389
You've gone off the deep end!

167
00:09:16,473 --> 00:09:18,433
You need to wake up, Rudersdorf!

168
00:09:18,516 --> 00:09:19,559
You're the one who--

169
00:09:19,642 --> 00:09:21,436
Is that head of yours
just for show?

170
00:09:21,519 --> 00:09:23,354
-Isn't there a brain in there?!
-Ugh.

171
00:09:23,438 --> 00:09:25,565
Think! You've gone too far!

172
00:09:26,441 --> 00:09:27,567
[pants]

173
00:09:27,650 --> 00:09:29,402
[KURT] We cannot lose this war.

174
00:09:30,069 --> 00:09:32,572
We have to justify
how many lives we've sacrificed.

175
00:09:32,655 --> 00:09:34,824
If we can't, then what do we
tell them back home?

176
00:09:37,160 --> 00:09:38,703
We tell them the truth.

177
00:09:39,996 --> 00:09:41,498
We tell them we're sorry.

178
00:09:41,581 --> 00:09:43,458
We tell them we failed.

179
00:09:44,918 --> 00:09:47,212
And we accept it
as they stone us to death.

180
00:09:48,671 --> 00:09:50,048
-[thwacks]
-[grunts]

181
00:09:50,131 --> 00:09:51,549
[objects clattering]

182
00:09:54,135 --> 00:09:55,637
[groans softly]

183
00:09:57,347 --> 00:09:58,640
You imbecile!

184
00:09:59,098 --> 00:10:00,225
Shut your mouth.

185
00:10:00,934 --> 00:10:03,728
You accuse me of not thinking,
then start spouting that?

186
00:10:04,395 --> 00:10:06,523
We must proudly give our all
to the Empire.

187
00:10:07,190 --> 00:10:09,067
Who cares if defeat is upon us?

188
00:10:09,776 --> 00:10:12,237
It's still our duty as soldiers
to win anyway.

189
00:10:13,154 --> 00:10:14,072
[sighs wearily]

190
00:10:15,115 --> 00:10:18,117
Is that it? Am I wrong? [scoffs]

191
00:10:19,244 --> 00:10:20,578
Oh, woe is me.

192
00:10:20,662 --> 00:10:21,955
Life sure is difficult

193
00:10:22,038 --> 00:10:23,790
when you've got an idiot
for a friend.

194
00:10:23,873 --> 00:10:24,874
You'd better watch out.

195
00:10:25,542 --> 00:10:28,461
I could use my new authority
as general to shut you up.

196
00:10:29,129 --> 00:10:30,004
[scoffs]

197
00:10:31,089 --> 00:10:33,174
Shall I be more respectful
talking to you?

198
00:10:33,258 --> 00:10:34,717
[scoffs] No need.

199
00:10:34,801 --> 00:10:36,094
I'm sure you got a promotion

200
00:10:36,177 --> 00:10:37,846
right around the same time
as me.

201
00:10:37,929 --> 00:10:40,139
Word travels awfully fast
around here.

202
00:10:41,099 --> 00:10:43,434
If I had known
how cheap these stars would feel

203
00:10:43,518 --> 00:10:44,978
when I put them on my chest,

204
00:10:45,061 --> 00:10:46,813
I might not have cared so much.

205
00:10:46,896 --> 00:10:48,147
It's sure something.

206
00:10:48,231 --> 00:10:50,191
I wonder how
Regiment Commander Laudon

207
00:10:50,275 --> 00:10:51,734
reacted when he heard the news.

208
00:10:51,818 --> 00:10:52,777
[chuckles]

209
00:10:52,861 --> 00:10:53,903
Good question.

210
00:10:54,279 --> 00:10:56,573
You think he broke out
the wake-up bugle for us?

211
00:10:56,656 --> 00:10:59,325
[KURT] Ha. [laughs]

212
00:11:01,244 --> 00:11:04,080
I can see I won't be able
to convince you this time.

213
00:11:04,163 --> 00:11:07,750
So, as your friend,
I'll do whatever I can to help.

214
00:11:07,834 --> 00:11:10,378
[smirks] Thanks. I owe you one.

215
00:11:11,004 --> 00:11:12,964
[engine sputtering]

216
00:11:17,302 --> 00:11:19,304
[thunder rumbling]

217
00:11:22,223 --> 00:11:23,433
[door opens]

218
00:11:23,516 --> 00:11:24,684
[TANYA] Excuse me, sir.

219
00:11:27,478 --> 00:11:29,230
That was General Rudersdorf.

220
00:11:29,314 --> 00:11:30,773
Has he been doing well?

221
00:11:32,066 --> 00:11:34,861
Yeah. As well as ever.

222
00:11:35,486 --> 00:11:37,780
Even the grip of death
couldn't change that man.

223
00:11:38,448 --> 00:11:40,366
Ah, somehow comforting to hear.

224
00:11:40,450 --> 00:11:41,868
So, sir,

225
00:11:41,951 --> 00:11:44,120
you called for me, yes?
What can I do?

226
00:11:45,163 --> 00:11:48,666
Of course.
I have an important task.

227
00:11:49,626 --> 00:11:52,170
Right. What exactly
would that be?

228
00:11:54,547 --> 00:11:56,883
My best friend
is causing trouble.

229
00:11:56,966 --> 00:11:58,593
[lightning crackling]

230
00:11:58,676 --> 00:12:00,595
You are to end his life.

231
00:12:00,678 --> 00:12:01,930
[thunderclap]

232
00:12:05,475 --> 00:12:07,936
[rain pattering heavily]

233
00:12:09,145 --> 00:12:10,939
[thunder rumbling]

234
00:12:12,065 --> 00:12:14,484
Pardon me? What did you say?

235
00:12:14,567 --> 00:12:16,986
I want you to paint
every wall of some room

236
00:12:17,070 --> 00:12:19,113
using my oldest friend's brains.

237
00:12:19,197 --> 00:12:21,115
I believe I was quite clear.

238
00:12:21,908 --> 00:12:24,118
Uh, may I ask you why, sir?

239
00:12:24,202 --> 00:12:27,580
Oh? You need a reason
to follow orders?

240
00:12:27,664 --> 00:12:29,582
When those orders
involve committing murder,

241
00:12:29,666 --> 00:12:30,875
then I'm afraid I do.

242
00:12:30,959 --> 00:12:32,627
I'm a soldier
who wishes to maintain

243
00:12:32,710 --> 00:12:33,836
some semblance of honor.

244
00:12:34,629 --> 00:12:37,548
In our world,
things work one way.

245
00:12:37,632 --> 00:12:40,051
He said he'd like
to flip it all on its head,

246
00:12:40,134 --> 00:12:42,345
so it's our duty
to keep it upright.

247
00:12:43,137 --> 00:12:45,932
He has the logic
of a military man,

248
00:12:46,015 --> 00:12:49,310
but that cannot come
before our national interest,

249
00:12:49,394 --> 00:12:51,437
The strategic
serving the tactical?

250
00:12:51,521 --> 00:12:53,189
-It's nonsense.
-[thunder rumbling]

251
00:12:53,856 --> 00:12:55,108
At the tactical level,

252
00:12:55,191 --> 00:12:57,568
I'm happy to fight
in pursuit of our victory.

253
00:12:57,652 --> 00:12:59,112
But to what end?

254
00:12:59,195 --> 00:13:02,615
If the Empire has no national
strategy, it's impossible.

255
00:13:03,533 --> 00:13:06,285
Even if we do win,
what would we do with it?

256
00:13:06,369 --> 00:13:08,663
Any accomplishments
would be meaningless.

257
00:13:10,206 --> 00:13:11,207
All right.

258
00:13:11,290 --> 00:13:12,834
Then let me turn that back
on you.

259
00:13:13,501 --> 00:13:16,004
What will you do next
if we manage to pull this off?

260
00:13:16,087 --> 00:13:17,463
What else is there to do

261
00:13:17,547 --> 00:13:20,508
but keep building the exit
our nation needs?

262
00:13:20,591 --> 00:13:22,510
Are you opposed
to Rudersdorf's plan

263
00:13:22,593 --> 00:13:24,595
to have the military
take control?

264
00:13:24,679 --> 00:13:26,681
No. Rather,

265
00:13:26,764 --> 00:13:28,683
the level of force
he's trying to use

266
00:13:28,766 --> 00:13:30,768
would hasten
the Empire's downfall.

267
00:13:31,477 --> 00:13:34,897
We must avoid chaos
no matter how this war ends.

268
00:13:41,029 --> 00:13:42,196
Colonel.

269
00:13:44,449 --> 00:13:45,742
Kill him.

270
00:13:46,701 --> 00:13:47,785
[Tanya grunts]

271
00:13:47,869 --> 00:13:49,328
[HANS] Perhaps in death,

272
00:13:49,412 --> 00:13:51,998
he'll finally make
field marshal.

273
00:13:56,252 --> 00:13:57,420
[muted dialogue]

274
00:14:02,300 --> 00:14:04,135
[TANYA]
What an absurd request to make.

275
00:14:08,389 --> 00:14:09,390
Why kill him?

276
00:14:09,474 --> 00:14:10,850
Because he's in your way?

277
00:14:10,933 --> 00:14:12,351
[scoffs in disbelief]
Unthinkable.

278
00:14:12,435 --> 00:14:14,145
[chuckles]
Absolutely out of the question.

279
00:14:14,771 --> 00:14:16,939
[HANS]
It's a necessary sacrifice.

280
00:14:17,023 --> 00:14:19,692
Are you really too soft-hearted
to kill an ally?

281
00:14:20,443 --> 00:14:22,904
[TANYA] Ah! My apologies.

282
00:14:22,987 --> 00:14:24,405
I think I led you astray

283
00:14:24,489 --> 00:14:26,949
since you're debating
the wrong part of my statement.

284
00:14:27,033 --> 00:14:28,785
Explain.

285
00:14:28,868 --> 00:14:31,537
You haven't caught on yet?
[chuckles]

286
00:14:31,621 --> 00:14:33,164
That's embarrassing, sir.

287
00:14:34,916 --> 00:14:36,042
Remember?

288
00:14:36,334 --> 00:14:39,087
"A soldier's corpse
is a tool for their country!"

289
00:14:39,170 --> 00:14:41,255
We can't throw them away
for nothing.

290
00:14:41,339 --> 00:14:42,340
[HANS] Hmm?

291
00:14:42,673 --> 00:14:44,300
[TANYA] If we just kill
General Rudersdorf,

292
00:14:44,384 --> 00:14:46,219
then his death is worthless.

293
00:14:46,302 --> 00:14:49,097
If a high-ranking officer
is going to die,

294
00:14:49,180 --> 00:14:51,182
then let's get some use
out of him.

295
00:14:52,350 --> 00:14:53,434
For instance,

296
00:14:53,518 --> 00:14:55,937
think of how exciting
it would be...

297
00:14:57,021 --> 00:14:59,315
...if he died
while committing treason.

298
00:15:00,566 --> 00:15:01,734
Interesting.

299
00:15:01,818 --> 00:15:03,361
You're suggesting
we could invent

300
00:15:03,444 --> 00:15:05,446
the circumstances postmortem?

301
00:15:05,530 --> 00:15:06,614
Yes.

302
00:15:07,198 --> 00:15:09,242
His death would be the catalyst
that would allow us to begin

303
00:15:09,325 --> 00:15:10,910
a selective purge of the army,

304
00:15:10,993 --> 00:15:12,745
and the outcry
from such a scandal

305
00:15:12,829 --> 00:15:15,373
would make a perfect excuse
to take control of the war.

306
00:15:16,332 --> 00:15:17,458
It sounds to me

307
00:15:17,542 --> 00:15:19,418
like you're proposing
a military coup.

308
00:15:19,502 --> 00:15:22,421
This can all be done
within the bounds of the law.

309
00:15:22,505 --> 00:15:24,382
We get one company
of MPs with us,

310
00:15:24,465 --> 00:15:27,427
we can easily arrest anyone
we deem to be his accomplice.

311
00:15:28,219 --> 00:15:29,846
From there, we can do anything.

312
00:15:31,305 --> 00:15:32,598
[HANS] Hmm.

313
00:15:32,682 --> 00:15:36,436
And if I agree,
how would we get this started?

314
00:15:37,103 --> 00:15:38,771
Ideally, we make it look like

315
00:15:38,855 --> 00:15:41,774
his death was a horrible
accident to the public.

316
00:15:41,858 --> 00:15:44,402
After that,
we find evidence of his plot

317
00:15:44,485 --> 00:15:45,778
while searching his belongings,

318
00:15:45,862 --> 00:15:47,905
and thus, the purge begins.

319
00:15:48,531 --> 00:15:49,740
Not bad.

320
00:15:49,824 --> 00:15:52,410
No, it's perfect. Although...

321
00:15:53,035 --> 00:15:55,455
there's one detail
it doesn't account for.

322
00:15:56,205 --> 00:15:57,165
Yes, you're right.

323
00:15:57,248 --> 00:15:58,916
For this plan to work,

324
00:15:58,999 --> 00:16:01,085
we need to find a man
who has enough strength

325
00:16:01,169 --> 00:16:04,213
and resolve to take full
military control of the war.

326
00:16:07,008 --> 00:16:08,968
[HANS]
I'm sacrificing my friend.

327
00:16:09,051 --> 00:16:11,971
Resolve is something
I've found in spades

328
00:16:12,054 --> 00:16:13,514
on the road to hell.

329
00:16:14,807 --> 00:16:16,934
-[grins]
-[thunderclap]

330
00:16:18,644 --> 00:16:19,770
All right, then.

331
00:16:19,854 --> 00:16:22,273
Soon, we can get this plan
underway.

332
00:16:22,899 --> 00:16:24,567
How do you suggest
we dispose of him?

333
00:16:24,650 --> 00:16:27,195
A plane crash would be easy
for the public to believe.

334
00:16:27,278 --> 00:16:30,114
A far too common event
in these times.

335
00:16:30,198 --> 00:16:31,282
Indeed.

336
00:16:31,365 --> 00:16:32,783
And since the war's start,

337
00:16:32,867 --> 00:16:35,828
we've been short on both parts
and mechanics.

338
00:16:35,912 --> 00:16:38,206
We'll need to make sure
it goes smooth.

339
00:16:38,289 --> 00:16:40,875
I'll send some mages I trust
that can see to that.

340
00:16:42,460 --> 00:16:44,503
The reputation of anyone
who's there

341
00:16:44,587 --> 00:16:46,088
is going to crash hard,

342
00:16:46,172 --> 00:16:47,673
[chuckles] not just the plane.

343
00:16:48,382 --> 00:16:50,885
And I simply feel terrible
about that fact.

344
00:16:50,968 --> 00:16:52,136
But if I may, sir...

345
00:16:52,762 --> 00:16:54,138
just look at your face.

346
00:16:54,805 --> 00:16:56,140
How can you object
to any of this

347
00:16:56,224 --> 00:16:57,225
with a grin like that?

348
00:16:57,308 --> 00:16:59,477
[thunder rumbling]

349
00:17:00,144 --> 00:17:01,354
-Lieutenant Colonel.
-Huh?

350
00:17:01,437 --> 00:17:03,898
I'm asking much of you
and your men.

351
00:17:03,981 --> 00:17:05,233
[chuckles smugly]

352
00:17:05,316 --> 00:17:08,277
You're damn right.
But hey, you always do.

353
00:17:09,278 --> 00:17:10,154
Very good.

354
00:17:10,238 --> 00:17:11,489
Well then,

355
00:17:11,572 --> 00:17:13,533
let us shed the blood
that needs to be shed.

356
00:17:14,158 --> 00:17:15,368
Sir!

357
00:17:15,451 --> 00:17:17,662
Look forward to good news
posthaste.

358
00:17:28,547 --> 00:17:30,508
[WILLIAM] You're sure?
Will this really work?

359
00:17:31,092 --> 00:17:32,552
[DRAKE] Impressive, no?

360
00:17:33,511 --> 00:17:35,388
Our bombers leave
from the kingdom

361
00:17:35,471 --> 00:17:37,265
and land in the Federation.

362
00:17:37,348 --> 00:17:39,475
When we're able to bomb them
from this far away,

363
00:17:39,559 --> 00:17:41,561
the Empire's got no chance
to keep up.

364
00:17:42,353 --> 00:17:43,980
On the surface,
it looks like another way

365
00:17:44,063 --> 00:17:46,023
to keep the pressure
going on them.

366
00:17:46,107 --> 00:17:47,775
They won't suspect our trick,

367
00:17:47,858 --> 00:17:49,652
that even these bombs
are just a decoy,

368
00:17:49,735 --> 00:17:52,571
so we can assassinate
an Imperial VIP.

369
00:17:52,655 --> 00:17:54,031
It still won't be easy.

370
00:17:54,115 --> 00:17:56,951
We can't let the Empire
find our inside man.

371
00:17:57,034 --> 00:18:00,204
You said we had a spy
deep in the Empire's heart?

372
00:18:00,288 --> 00:18:02,248
It's a secret,
I understand that,

373
00:18:02,331 --> 00:18:03,833
but you're asking a lot.

374
00:18:03,916 --> 00:18:06,168
I know it requires trust
from your men,

375
00:18:06,252 --> 00:18:08,713
but General Rudersdorf's head
will be well worth it.

376
00:18:09,338 --> 00:18:10,589
[William grunts softly]

377
00:18:10,673 --> 00:18:13,092
That said,
I have one more favor to ask.

378
00:18:13,718 --> 00:18:15,845
No one can suspect anything.

379
00:18:15,928 --> 00:18:18,514
And that means even
keeping our friends in the dark.

380
00:18:18,598 --> 00:18:21,642
You want him dead without
the Federation finding out?

381
00:18:22,393 --> 00:18:24,937
I don't care how you do it,
just find a way to cover it up.

382
00:18:25,604 --> 00:18:28,274
-Now, I'll see myself out.
-Hold on.

383
00:18:29,400 --> 00:18:31,444
-[MIKEL] A shuttle bombing?
-[LILIYA] Yes.

384
00:18:31,527 --> 00:18:34,322
Allied Kingdom bombers
will strike the Empire

385
00:18:34,405 --> 00:18:35,865
and land on our soil.

386
00:18:36,532 --> 00:18:38,284
They'll resupply
and go back home,

387
00:18:38,367 --> 00:18:39,869
dropping more bombs on the way.

388
00:18:41,037 --> 00:18:43,456
This is a united effort
to attack the Empire,

389
00:18:43,539 --> 00:18:45,291
a demonstration of friendship.

390
00:18:46,083 --> 00:18:48,252
International friendship
is good for us.

391
00:18:48,336 --> 00:18:50,379
So, what orders
does the party have

392
00:18:50,463 --> 00:18:52,840
-for me in particular?
-Simple.

393
00:18:52,923 --> 00:18:55,051
We want you
to welcome our friends.

394
00:18:55,134 --> 00:18:56,594
In basic terms,

395
00:18:56,677 --> 00:18:58,304
you'd like me to guide
the bombers to the base

396
00:18:58,387 --> 00:18:59,555
and keep them safe.

397
00:19:00,014 --> 00:19:00,931
Understood.

398
00:19:01,015 --> 00:19:02,516
In addition, comrade,

399
00:19:02,600 --> 00:19:04,977
the Federation has prepared
a surprise gift,

400
00:19:05,061 --> 00:19:07,104
something for our friends
after their long trip.

401
00:19:07,188 --> 00:19:09,440
-What kind of surprise?
-[LILIYA] More security.

402
00:19:11,025 --> 00:19:13,986
We have a new computation jewel
for our ace mages.

403
00:19:14,612 --> 00:19:15,571
The surprise will be

404
00:19:16,072 --> 00:19:18,366
how much more potent
their escort is than expected.

405
00:19:19,533 --> 00:19:20,701
[MIKEL] I see.

406
00:19:20,993 --> 00:19:23,621
We want a show of strength
to keep them on our side.

407
00:19:24,664 --> 00:19:27,666
A tall order when I'm not
used to these jewels,

408
00:19:27,750 --> 00:19:29,418
plus, they're unstable.

409
00:19:29,502 --> 00:19:30,503
But...

410
00:19:30,586 --> 00:19:32,213
If this is what the party wants,

411
00:19:32,838 --> 00:19:34,757
it's my honor as always to obey.

412
00:19:34,840 --> 00:19:36,842
Let's see what these can do.

413
00:19:42,223 --> 00:19:43,933
You're sure
this is what you want to do?

414
00:19:45,559 --> 00:19:46,435
Sir?

415
00:19:47,687 --> 00:19:48,854
Give me a moment.

416
00:19:49,688 --> 00:19:51,857
He's still my dearest friend.

417
00:19:51,941 --> 00:19:53,734
A part of me still wants to see

418
00:19:53,818 --> 00:19:55,569
if I could talk him
off the edge.

419
00:19:56,946 --> 00:19:58,697
I must steel my heart.

420
00:19:58,781 --> 00:20:00,908
I thought it had hardened
long ago.

421
00:20:01,700 --> 00:20:02,660
I'll be blunt, sir.

422
00:20:03,327 --> 00:20:04,870
We're nothing more than human.

423
00:20:06,539 --> 00:20:08,499
I'm surprised to hear that
from you.

424
00:20:08,582 --> 00:20:10,709
Are you sure?
Think you're still human?

425
00:20:10,793 --> 00:20:12,878
I take great pride in that, sir.

426
00:20:12,962 --> 00:20:14,672
I bow to no god or devil.

427
00:20:14,755 --> 00:20:17,258
And if any of them
ever try to get in my way,

428
00:20:17,341 --> 00:20:18,926
then I would gladly crush them

429
00:20:19,009 --> 00:20:21,095
using the full force
of the human race,

430
00:20:21,178 --> 00:20:22,430
as is my right.

431
00:20:23,472 --> 00:20:24,432
Of course.

432
00:20:25,099 --> 00:20:25,975
As for me,

433
00:20:26,642 --> 00:20:30,563
I've had to burn away
the last dregs of my humanity.

434
00:20:31,313 --> 00:20:33,315
♪

435
00:21:03,137 --> 00:21:04,013
Hmm.

436
00:21:08,851 --> 00:21:11,270
I have one more request
I need to make of you.

437
00:21:14,440 --> 00:21:15,941
-[hinges creaking]
-[thuds]

438
00:21:28,370 --> 00:21:29,288
Hmm.

439
00:21:40,883 --> 00:21:42,885
[plane roaring]

440
00:21:50,351 --> 00:21:53,062
[TANYA] They always save
the shittiest job for last

441
00:21:53,145 --> 00:21:54,522
before you get to quit.

442
00:21:55,231 --> 00:21:57,608
But if I want
to make a clean exit...

443
00:22:00,361 --> 00:22:02,238
...I have no choice.

444
00:22:11,038 --> 00:22:13,415
[♪ closing theme song playing]

